# Dietary identity and the neural categorization of food images: an open EEG dataset with Food-pics stimuli

## Overview
14-channel EEG (EMOTIV EPOC X; four recordings, sub-01, sub-02, sub-03 and sub-09,
on the earlier EPOC+ model; scalp positions AF3 F7 F3 FC5 T7 P7 O1 O2 P8 T8 FC6
F4 F8 AF4) recorded from 77 adults while they categorized prepared-food images
as meat or vegetarian. Each recording also includes a short eyes-open /
eyes-closed resting baseline that precedes the task. Stimuli were presented and
event markers logged by the EmotivLABS web-based experiment platform (EMOTIV
Builder). Images are from the Food-pics database (Blechert et al., 2014, 2019).

These data were collected within longer sessions that also recorded other
measures (reported elsewhere). Only the categorization paradigm and its resting
baseline are released here.

## Task
The same set of 37 prepared-food images was presented twice (up to ~74 trials).
On each trial a participant pressed M (meat) or X (vegetarian). Participants
were instructed that accuracy mattered more than speed. Response times were
recorded.

In the events files:
- `stim_category` is the displayed image's true category: m (meat) or v (vegetarian).
- `response_key` is the physical key pressed: M or X.
- `response` is that key mapped back to category (m/v) so it lines up with `stim_category`.
- `accuracy` is 1 when `response` matches `stim_category`.
- `boundary` rows mark the two segment joins (see "Recording structure").

## Files
- `participants.tsv` / `participants.json`: one row per participant, with
  demographics, baseline questionnaire (verbatim answers; the JSON gives the
  question wording and response options), dietary-preference profile (verbatim
  answer, binary exclusion indicators, derived category), and per-subject task
  summaries. `.tsv` is tab-separated and opens like a CSV.
- `sub-NN/eeg/`: the recording (`_eeg.edf`), `_events.tsv`, `_channels.tsv`,
  `_eeg.json` sidecar, and standard 10-20 electrode positions
  (`_electrodes.tsv`, `_coordsystem.json`).
- `derivatives/eeglab/`: the same data as EEGLAB `.set` files with labelled
  events (see the README there). `code/`: the script that generates them.
- `stimuli/foodpics_stimuli.tsv` / `.json`: the 37 Food-pics images used in the
  task (19 meat, 18 vegetarian), with their Food-pics image number and
  description, their category in the task, and their Food-pics normative
  familiarity (percentage of omnivore raters familiar with the food). The image
  files themselves are available from the Food-pics database, not from here.

## Recording structure
Each EDF joins three segments of the source recording: a 30-s eyes-open window
(0-30 s), a 30-s eyes-closed window (30-60 s) and the categorization task (60 s
to the end). The resting segments advanced on the participant's button press, so
a fixed 30-s window inside each instructed segment is released. Each join is a
discontinuity and is marked by a `boundary` event (in `events.tsv` and as an EDF+
annotation); filter each segment separately, or use software that respects
boundary events (EEGLAB does so natively).

## Channels and data quality
Each EDF holds 46 channels:
- 14 EEG channels (µV).
- `CQ_<sensor>` (14): EMOTIV contact quality, impedance-based, coded 0 (very
  bad), 1 (bad), 2 (ok) and 4 (good); the device does not use the value 3.
- `EQ_<sensor>` (14): EMOTIV EEG quality, 0-4, the minimum of contact quality,
  a signal-quality score and a signal-magnitude score, each over 2-s windows.
  Low impedance alone does not guarantee a usable signal, so EQ is the more
  informative index for screening.
- `CQ_CMS`, `CQ_DRL`: contact quality of the two reference sensors.
- `CQ_OVERALL`, `EQ_OVERALL`: overall indices in percent.
All quality channels are typed MISC in `channels.tsv`, which also gives each EEG
sensor's mean CQ and EQ over the recording. Contact quality was uniformly high
across the released recordings; EEG quality varied more between and within
sessions, so re-users can set their own thresholds per channel, per time window
or per participant.

The resting baseline supports a per-participant eyes-closed alpha-reactivity
check (`berger_alpha_ratio`, `berger_pass` in `participants.tsv`); 12 of 77
recordings do not show the expected increase and are retained.

## Processing
Data are released minimally processed: no re-referencing, artifact rejection,
ICA or filtering beyond the anti-aliasing step described below. The device applies its built-in filtering (0.16-43 Hz
bandwidth, 5th-order sinc filter, 50/60 Hz notch). All recordings are provided
at 128 Hz; where the headset recorded at 256 Hz, the signal was downsampled with
a zero-phase anti-aliasing low-pass filter (cutoff 51.2 Hz, above the headset's
43 Hz bandwidth), so all recordings are comparable within that bandwidth.

## Event timing
Event markers were generated in software by the web-based platform; their
latency and jitter relative to the on-screen stimulus were not measured
independently (no photodiode). Analyses of spectral power, resting-state
activity, behaviour and slow or late components are little affected. Analyses
of early, short-latency event-related potentials are the most sensitive to
marker jitter and should take it into account (see Williams et al., 2021,
https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.10700).

## Notes for re-use
- Read the EDFs with a reader that parses the EMOTIV header correctly, for
  example pyedflib, EEGLAB (BIOSIG) or the provided EEGLAB `.set` files; some
  general-purpose readers return flat signals for these files.
- Recording dates have been removed; files carry a neutral fixed date. No names
  or device identifiers are included.
- Keypresses other than M or X are coded `response_key` = other and scored
  `accuracy` = n/a (43 trials in total). Forty-one of them come from one
  participant, sub-52, whose keypresses were registered by the platform with a
  non-standard key code on 41 of 68 trials: on 31 of 33 vegetarian images and
  on 10 of 35 meat images. This code probably stood in for the X key in most,
  but not all, cases. The `mean_accuracy` for sub-52 in `participants.tsv` is
  therefore based on only 27 trials; re-users may wish to exclude sub-52 from
  analyses of accuracy or response time.

